WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.074 --> 00:00:09.216
thousand years ago, the ancient Greeks introduced a new sports competition.

2
00:00:09.996 --> 00:00:14.058
There were many athletic contests in ancient Greece, but this one was different.

3
00:00:14.698 --> 00:00:20.941
For a start, this competition honored the mighty Zeus, ruler of all Greek gods, so it included not

4
00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:23.542
only sports, but religious celebration as well.

5
00:00:24.382 --> 00:00:30.445
For the next thousand years, it attracted the best athletes from Greek city-states, from places as far away

6
00:00:30.505 --> 00:00:32.867
as modern-day Ukraine, Egypt, and Turkey.

7
00:00:33.888 --> 00:00:39.232
The first of these contests took place in the Elis region of Greece, at a sacred spot called

8
00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:41.854
Olympia, in 776 BC.

9
00:00:42.694 --> 00:00:48.399
The competition was so successful that the Greeks continued to hold similar events every four years.

10
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.942
They called these competitions the Olympic Games.

11
00:00:52.762 --> 00:00:54.784
So why were the Games so special?

12
00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.141
Exercise was very important in the ancient Greek world.

13
00:00:59.942 --> 00:01:05.924
The Greeks believed in a well-rounded education, emphasizing exercise for the body as well as the mind.

14
00:01:06.765 --> 00:01:10.846
Moreover they believed that men should stay in shape in case they had to serve in the army.

15
00:01:11.487 --> 00:01:16.168
To promote health, every Greek city had a gymnasium and a wrestling school called a palaestra.

16
00:01:16.769 --> 00:01:22.931
These places were so important to each community that they also became social centers, where citizens could meet

17
00:01:23.171 --> 00:01:26.277
to discuss politics or listen to educational lectures.

18
00:01:26.997 --> 00:01:33.039
The Olympics celebrated the importance of fitness in Greek society by providing a forum for the best athletes

19
00:01:33.079 --> 00:01:34.839
to come together and flaunt their skills.

20
00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:38.400
These people proudly represented their local communities.

21
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:44.582
If someone from Athens, for example, won an event, he brought honor to his entire city-state.

22
00:01:45.602 --> 00:01:48.743
The Olympic Games also had some political benefits for the Greeks.

23
00:01:49.463 --> 00:01:54.209
In the months before the Games started, messengers went from city to city announcing the dates of the

24
00:01:54.269 --> 00:01:57.070
event and that the Olympic Truce was to begin.

25
00:01:57.751 --> 00:02:03.793
The Olympic Truce stated that anyone involved in the Games must be allowed safe passage to Olympia, even

26
00:02:03.833 --> 00:02:05.554
through hostile enemy territory.

27
00:02:06.294 --> 00:02:13.617
The Olympic Games brought together residents of numerous city-states, allowing them to discuss politics, make alliances, and even

28
00:02:13.657 --> 00:02:14.678
negotiate peace.

29
00:02:15.478 --> 00:02:19.520
Meanwhile athletes were training hard for the upcoming games with their coaches.

30
00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:25.585
Each city selected its best athletes to travel to Elis one month ahead of the Games to train

31
00:02:25.665 --> 00:02:28.407
on site, where they stayed in huge tents.

32
00:02:29.268 --> 00:02:36.074
During this period Olympic officials, who chose which athletes qualified to compete, evaluated all hopeful contestants.

33
00:02:36.854 --> 00:02:42.859
Those selected competed individually, not as part of a team, and competition was limited to Greek men.

34
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:48.264
Slaves, foreigners, and unmarried women could only participate as spectators.

35
00:02:48.928 --> 00:02:53.312
They joined the thousands of Greek men who traveled from far away to see the Games.

36
00:02:54.052 --> 00:02:59.697
As many as fifty thousand spectators and merchants attended the Games, camping outside under the stars.

37
00:03:00.558 --> 00:03:03.620
Married women, however, were prohibited from viewing the Games.

38
00:03:04.421 --> 00:03:08.744
If one was caught on the grounds, she was thrown off a nearby mountain as punishment!

39
00:03:09.605 --> 00:03:13.148
The Olympic Festival began with an opening ceremony in a temple.

40
00:03:13.889 --> 00:03:17.832
This rite included the sacrifice of one hundred heads of cattle to honor Zeus.

41
00:03:18.576 --> 00:03:23.921
Afterwards poets and playwrights presented their works, the crowds cheered, and the Games began.

42
00:03:25.422 --> 00:03:31.147
All events took place either in the stadium or the hippodrome, a track for racing horses.

43
00:03:32.168 --> 00:03:38.053
The very first Olympic Games lasted only three days, but by the fifth century BC, they had been

44
00:03:38.093 --> 00:03:39.334
extended to five days.

45
00:03:40.014 --> 00:03:43.637
The spectators sat on the ground, while officials watched from tents.

46
00:03:44.518 --> 00:03:50.608
At first the main event was a footrace, but over time more competitions were added, such as boxing,

47
00:03:50.828 --> 00:03:52.169
wrestling, and chariot racing.

48
00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:56.012
The events back then were a little different from their modern counterparts.

49
00:03:56.693 --> 00:04:00.376
Boxing, for example, had no rule against hitting an opponent while he was down.

50
00:04:01.297 --> 00:04:06.362
The footrace sometimes became so violent that the athletes were forced to wear helmets and carry shields.

51
00:04:07.163 --> 00:04:12.828
Another modern development is uniforms; in the ancient Olympics, athletes competed totally nude.

52
00:04:13.644 --> 00:04:18.687
They did this because the Greeks had a deep appreciation for the human body and believed that displaying

53
00:04:18.728 --> 00:04:19.888
it was a tribute to Zeus.

54
00:04:20.949 --> 00:04:26.813
Another difference between the ancient and modern Olympics is that there was only one winner in each eventㅡthere

55
00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:32.076
was no second or third place─and he received an olive branch as his prize instead of a medal.

56
00:04:32.797 --> 00:04:37.620
This may not seem like much, but a victorious athlete could live off his achievements for the rest

57
00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:38.180
of his life.

58
00:04:38.821 --> 00:04:45.554
His hometown might reward him with food, cash, honorary appointments, or leadership positions in the community.

59
00:04:46.194 --> 00:04:51.958
Greek writers, poets, and sculptors also recorded his story, so that his victory was never forgotten.

60
00:04:52.939 --> 00:04:58.603
One such athlete was the boxer Melankomas of Caria, a town in modern Turkey.

61
00:04:59.303 --> 00:05:01.685
He won the boxing event in 49 AD.

62
00:05:02.385 --> 00:05:07.409
He was famous not only for his magnificent body and good looks, but also for his unusual fighting

63
00:05:07.449 --> 00:05:14.229
technique. Melankomas did not strike his opponents; rather, he wore them out by skillfully dodging their blows.

64
00:05:15.069 --> 00:05:19.531
It was said that he could fight all day long without tiring, even in the Greek heat.

65
00:05:20.472 --> 00:05:25.774
Another legendary athlete of the ancient world was Milo of Kroton, who was from what is now Italy.

66
00:05:26.454 --> 00:05:31.936
He won the wrestling event at six Olympic Games between 540 BC and 516 BC.

67
00:05:32.617 --> 00:05:36.058
Milo enjoyed showing off his unrivaled strength and control.

68
00:05:36.698 --> 00:05:40.040
He would hold a piece of fruit in his hand and challenge people to take it away from

69
00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:44.998
him. Even though he held the fruit so tightly that no one could take it, he never damaged

70
00:05:45.038 --> 00:05:50.801
it. Another of his favorite tricks was to tie a string around his head, hold his breath, and

71
00:05:50.841 --> 00:05:53.943
break the string with the veins bulging out of his forehead!

72
00:05:54.644 --> 00:05:58.506
Theagenes of Thasos became famous at the age of nine.

73
00:05:59.326 --> 00:06:04.149
One day, as he was walking home from school, he noticed a bronze statue of a god in

74
00:06:04.169 --> 00:06:04.929
the marketplace.

75
00:06:05.569 --> 00:06:09.992
For some inexplicable reason, Theagenes broke the statue off its base.

76
00:06:10.764 --> 00:06:15.626
The story of a young boy who could break bronze with his bare hands was talked about everywhere.

77
00:06:16.326 --> 00:06:21.188
When he was older, Theagenes competed in the Olympics and easily won the boxing prize.

78
00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:25.690
Other athletes were remembered for less honorable reasons.

79
00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:30.912
Even though all contestants in the Games took an oath to Zeus that they would abide by the

80
00:06:30.952 --> 00:06:33.273
rules, some did not keep this promise.

81
00:06:34.254 --> 00:06:39.956
One infamous cheater was Eupolus of Thessaly, who bribed other boxers in the 98th Olympics.

82
00:06:40.756 --> 00:06:46.478
In the 112th Olympics, Callippus of Athens gave money to all the other athletes in his race to

83
00:06:46.518 --> 00:06:47.099
let him win.

84
00:06:48.059 --> 00:06:51.520
Cheating was punished; dishonest athletes had to pay huge fines.

85
00:06:52.221 --> 00:06:57.163
With this money, statues of Zeus were erected along the road that led to the stadium, with the

86
00:06:57.223 --> 00:06:59.464
names of the cheats inscribed on their bases.

87
00:07:00.284 --> 00:07:05.146
To get to the stadium, the athletes had to walk past all the statues, which reminded them of

88
00:07:05.206 --> 00:07:06.086
what not to do.

89
00:07:07.347 --> 00:07:12.610
The Olympic Games were so important in the ancient Greek world that time was not measured in years,

90
00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:13.951
but in Olympiads.

91
00:07:14.751 --> 00:07:18.895
One Olympiad is the amount of time between each Olympic Game, four years.

92
00:07:19.595 --> 00:07:23.518
Things changed, however, in 146 BC, when the Romans conquered Greece.

93
00:07:24.059 --> 00:07:28.182
They did not worship Zeus, the Romans were Christian.

94
00:07:28.282 --> 00:07:35.628
In 393 AD Roman Emperor Theodosius I abolished the Olympic Games because they did not want their citizens

95
00:07:35.668 --> 00:07:37.170
celebrating a pagan god.

96
00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:43.933
It wasn't until the late nineteenth century that people started to think again about organizing an international sporting

97
00:07:43.993 --> 00:07:46.494
event and reinvented the Olympic Games.

98
00:07:47.254 --> 00:07:53.176
Today, almost two thousand years after the original Games ended, athletes from most countries in the world participate

99
00:07:53.216 --> 00:07:59.218
in the modern Olympics, a tribute to the lasting influence of the Ancient Greek heroes, their traditions, and

100
00:07:59.298 --> 00:08:00.058
eternal spirit.
